@model Edulytics.Core.Curriculum.LessonContent.RichLessonV2

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L01")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l01 aria-label="Grade 7 scaled flags and percentages">
    <h3>Scaled Flags and Percent Area — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>1.1 Preserve shape while changing size</h4>
    <p>Compare rectangular flags with different dimensions. Decide which are scaled copies by checking a common multiplicative factor for corresponding lengths.</p>
    <h4>1.2 Percent of a rectangular region</h4>
    <p>For a flag with a rectangular subregion, express the subregion area as a fraction of the whole and convert that fraction to a percentage.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>A 6-by-10 flag is scaled so its width becomes 15. Find the new height. A 3-by-4 rectangle inside the original flag is highlighted. What percentage of the original flag area is highlighted? Explain why scaling the entire flag preserves that percentage.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the scale-factor explanation, area model and justification that corresponding area fractions are preserved. Machine Practice checks exact scale-factor and fraction-to-percent subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L02")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l02 aria-label="Grade 7 fractional ratios and rates">
    <h3>Rates with Fractions — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>2.1 Compare by one unit</h4><p>Convert fractional distance-time and recipe ratios to unit rates. Keep units attached to every quotient and compare rates only after the units agree.</p>
    <h4>2.2 Scale a proportional image</h4><p>Use a fractional scale factor to find missing dimensions and explain why multiplying corresponding lengths by one factor preserves the shape.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>Runner A travels 2.25 miles in 18 minutes. Runner B travels 1.75 miles in 15 minutes. Find each rate in miles per minute and decide who is faster. Then state the reciprocal rate for the faster runner in minutes per mile.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check unit interpretation, comparison reasoning and the reciprocal-rate explanation. Machine Practice checks exact fractional rate subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L03")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l03 aria-label="Grade 7 revisiting proportional relationships">
    <h3>Revisit Proportional Relationships — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>3.1 Move among representations</h4><p>For one constant-rate situation, identify the unit rate, complete a table, write an equation, and explain what the constant means in context.</p>
    <h4>3.2 Use the inverse rate when useful</h4><p>When a problem asks how much input is needed for one output, switch to the reciprocal unit rate and state its units explicitly.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>Bulk walnuts cost 18 for 7.5 pounds. Find dollars per pound and pounds per dollar, write an equation for cost c in terms of pounds p, and find the cost of 12 pounds.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check units, interpretation of the constant and the choice of forward versus reciprocal rate. Machine Practice checks exact rate, table and equation subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L04")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l04 aria-label="Grade 7 fractional increases and decreases">
    <h3>Describe Changes with Fractions — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>4.1 Connect words, addition, and multiplication</h4><p>Translate statements such as "half as much again" into both additive and multiplicative forms, and explain why x + 1/2 x = 3/2 x.</p>
    <h4>4.2 Increase versus decrease</h4><p>Use tape diagrams or equations to distinguish adding a fraction of the original from subtracting a fraction of the original. Keep the original quantity as the reference.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A skating route is 24 km. A longer route is 3/8 more than the original and a shorter route is 1/4 less than the original. Find both route lengths and write one equation for each change using the original length.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the reference quantity, equation interpretation, and explanation of why the multiplier is 1 plus or minus the fractional change. Machine Practice checks exact fractional-change subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L05")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l05 aria-label="Grade 7 decimal change factors">
    <h3>Say the Change with Decimals — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>5.1 Convert the change, not the reference</h4><p>Convert a fractional increase or decrease to a decimal, then combine it with 1 to form the multiplier on the original amount.</p>
    <h4>5.2 Explain equivalent equations</h4><p>Connect forms such as x+0.5x and 1.5x, and distinguish a terminating decimal from a repeating decimal when converting fractions.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A reader finishes 80 pages. A second reader finishes 0.375 more than that amount. Find the second amount, write both additive and multiplicative equations, and state the fraction equivalent to 0.375.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check equivalence among fraction, decimal, diagram and equation representations. Machine Practice checks exact conversion and change-factor subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L06")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l06 aria-label="Grade 7 percent increase and decrease">
    <h3>Percent Increase and Decrease — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>6.1 Keep 100% anchored to the original</h4><p>Use a tape diagram to show an original amount as 100%, then add or remove the stated percentage of that original amount.</p>
    <h4>6.2 Separate change from final amount</h4><p>Calculate the percent-change amount first, then combine it with the original. Explain why the final amount is more or less than 100% of the original.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A fish population is 640. It decreases by 15%. Find the decrease and the new population. Then write the new population as a percentage of the original.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the reference quantity, tape diagram and distinction between percent change and final percent. Machine Practice checks exact percentage calculations only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L07")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l07 aria-label="Grade 7 recover the original amount">
    <h3>Find the 100% Reference — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>7.1 Work backward from a changed amount</h4><p>When a final value represents 110%, 80%, or another percentage of the original, divide by the multiplier to recover the 100% reference.</p>
    <h4>7.2 Use a double number line</h4><p>Place the final percentage and final amount together, then scale to 100%. Check the result by applying the change forward again.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A detergent bottle now holds 660 mL after a 10% increase. Find the original volume. A second product is 480 g after a 20% decrease. Find its original mass and verify both answers forward.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check which quantity is the 100% reference and the backward reasoning. Machine Practice checks exact reverse-percent subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L08")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l08 aria-label="Grade 7 percent change equations">
    <h3>Percent Change with Equations — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>8.1 Build the multiplier</h4><p>An increase by p% uses 1+p/100; a decrease uses 1-p/100. Explain the multiplier before substituting numbers.</p>
    <h4>8.2 Read an equation backward</h4><p>Given a final value and a percent-change equation, divide by the multiplier to recover the original amount.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A savings bond worth 500 increases by 8%. Write an equation for the new value and calculate it. A device is worth 540 after a 10% depreciation; find its value before depreciation.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check equation meaning and reference quantity. Machine Practice checks exact forward and reverse multiplier calculations only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L09")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l09 aria-label="Grade 7 nonwhole percentages">
    <h3>Percentages below 1% and between Whole Percents — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>9.1 Interpret the decimal point in a percent</h4><p>Convert 0.1%, 0.4%, 5.125%, and 12.5% to decimal multipliers by dividing the percentage by 100.</p>
    <h4>9.2 Use benchmarks</h4><p>Find 1% first when useful, then scale it to a fraction of 1% or to a mixed decimal percent.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>Find 0.4% of 2,500 and 12.5% of 80. Explain why both answers are 10 even though the percentages and base quantities differ.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check decimal-place reasoning and percent-to-multiplier explanations. Machine Practice checks exact nonwhole-percentage values only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L10")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l10 aria-label="Grade 7 tax and tip">
    <h3>Tax and Tip — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>10.1 Separate subtotal, added amount, and total</h4><p>Compute tax or tip from the subtotal, then add it once. When the added amount is known, divide by the subtotal to recover the percentage.</p>
    <h4>10.2 Handle nonwhole rates</h4><p>Use decimal tax rates such as 7.25% without rounding the rate early. Round money only at the point required by the context.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A purchase has a 64.00 subtotal and 7.25% sales tax. Find the tax and total. A restaurant bill is 40.00 and the tip is 7.40; find the tip percentage.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check money rounding, reference subtotal, and distinction between added amount and total. Machine Practice checks exact percent/rate subresults before currency rounding.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L11")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l11 aria-label="Grade 7 percentage contexts">
    <h3>Markup, Discount, Commission, and Interest — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>11.1 Name the base before calculating</h4><p>For each context, identify what amount represents 100% before choosing a percent multiplier or reverse calculation.</p>
    <h4>11.2 Reverse a discount or markup</h4><p>A discounted or marked-up price is a percentage of the original. Divide by that final multiplier to recover the original price.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>An item sells for 76 after a 20% discount. Find the original price. A salesperson earns 5.5% commission on a 600 sale; find the commission and the amount retained by the store before other costs.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check base-quantity selection and interpretation of what each result represents. Machine Practice checks exact reverse-percent and commission subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L12")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l12 aria-label="Grade 7 finding the percentage">
    <h3>Find the Unknown Percentage — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>12.1 Difference first, reference second</h4><p>Find the change between two values, divide by the original reference amount, then multiply by 100 to obtain the percent change.</p>
    <h4>12.2 Ask for enough information</h4><p>In an information-gap problem, identify which original and changed quantities are required before calculating a percentage.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A restaurant bill is 42 and the final amount after tip is 49.77. Find the tip amount and tip percentage. A pair of shoes drops from 80 to 68; find the percent decrease.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check selection of the original reference and information requests. Machine Practice checks exact percent-from-values subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L13")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l13 aria-label="Grade 7 measurement error">
    <h3>Measurement Error — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>13.1 Separate absolute error from percent error</h4><p>Absolute error is the distance between measured and actual values. Percent error compares that distance with the actual value.</p>
    <h4>13.2 Connect precision to possible error</h4><p>A measurement rounded to a stated unit represents an interval of possible actual values; explain how tool precision and reading technique affect the result.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>An actual length is 80 cm and a measurement reads 76 cm. Find the absolute error and percent error. Give two plausible sources of measurement error that could produce a difference.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check plausible measurement-error sources and correct use of the actual value as denominator. Machine Practice checks exact percent-error subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L14")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l14 aria-label="Grade 7 percent error">
    <h3>Percent Error in Context — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>14.1 Use absolute difference</h4><p>Percent error uses the magnitude of the error, so an overestimate and an underestimate of the same size have the same percent error when the actual value is the same.</p>
    <h4>14.2 Reverse an error statement</h4><p>When a problem gives the actual value and an error percentage, use the stated direction if known to reconstruct a possible measured or estimated value.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A package actually weighs 250 g but is measured as 243 g. Find the percent error. Then state whether a 2.8% underestimate is consistent with those values.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check use of absolute error, denominator choice and direction language. Machine Practice checks exact percent-error and reverse-percent subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L15")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l15 aria-label="Grade 7 error intervals">
    <h3>Error Intervals — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>15.1 Build the interval from the target</h4><p>Multiply the target value by the maximum percent error, then subtract and add that error to obtain the lower and upper acceptable bounds.</p>
    <h4>15.2 Interpret a tolerance rule</h4><p>Decide whether a measured value lies inside the interval and explain why the same absolute error can matter differently for small and large target values.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A target length is 80 cm with a maximum error of 2.5%. Find the acceptable interval. Decide whether measurements of 77.8 cm, 78 cm, and 82.1 cm are acceptable.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check endpoint interpretation, inclusive/exclusive wording and quality-control reasoning. Machine Practice checks exact interval endpoints and percent-error subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}
